  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.3. Increased capacity of Member States to set adequate wages and promote decent working time</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 29 September 2022, Government Decision No. 670  was approved. It established a unified minimum wage covering the public and private sectors. On 1 January, 2023, the minimum wage was increased to 4,000 MDL (roughly, 200 US$,) in both sectors, with potential for further increase at sector levels through effective collective bargaining. This reform has resulted in an increase in earnings for about 120,000 wage employees located at the low end of the wage distribution; these represent about 17 percent of wage employees (about 13.5 percent of all workers in Moldova). Moreover, the new policy has a significant effect at reducing gender inequality because most of those that benefit from the policy are women low paid; in fact, about 51,000 of the 120,000 who will benefit from an increase in earnings (i.e., 65.3 percent) are women. The ILO supported the Tripartite Commission for Consultancy and Collective Bargaining (NCCCB) on their discussions of minimum wages by producing  an empirical assessment and provided recommendations to reinforcing the minimum wage fixing procedures through social dialogue, as well as strengthening legal measures for evidence-based minimum wage setting.
Between June and November 2022, the ILO drafted a report on new minimum wage fixing procedures in Moldova, requested by the Ministry of Labour and Social Protection. On 1 December 2022, the report findings and recommendations were validated in a meeting of the NCCCB.</narrative>
   </description>
